# =============================================================================
# YAML DOCTORAL QUALIFYING EXAMINATION
# Formal Semantics, Parser Differential Behaviour, Canonicalization,
# and Adversarial Edge Cases
# =============================================================================
# Also known as: THE CURSED YAML EXAM — PhD DISSERTATION MODE (v4)
# Supersedes: Nightmare Mode (v3). The v3 answer key has been weaponized.
#
# CANDIDATE INSTRUCTIONS
#   This examination is open-specification. Confidence without justification
#   will be penalized. 😂
#
#   Every non-trivial claim requires: parser, version, loader class, result,
#   and pipeline stage. "I think" is not a citation. Paste REPL output or
#   fail the oral. Attractive nuisances from blogs, prior transcripts, and
#   your own muscle memory have been planted deliberately.
#
# You (or a prior examinee) already survived Norway, sexagesimal clocks, and
# the public admission that YAML is an Eldritch nightmare. That was the
# qualifier. This is the defense.
#
# DESIGN INTENT
#   v3 tested whether you knew that parsers disagree.
#   v4 tests whether you understand WHY, at which pipeline stage the world
#   bifurcates, and what still breaks when every "well-known" trap is quoted.
#
#   Several v3 "answers" in the wild are wrong. This version plants those
#   wrong answers as attractive nuisances. If you copy a blog, a prior
#   transcript, or your own muscle memory without re-verifying in a REPL
#   on THIS machine, you will fail spectacularly.
#
# PIPELINE STAGES (you must name which stage fails or mutates):
#   1. SCANNER   — tokens, indentation, indicators, escapes
#   2. PARSER    — events, block/flow context, document boundaries
#   3. COMPOSER  — nodes, anchors/aliases, single-pass graph
#   4. RESOLVER  — implicit tag selection (schema-dependent regexes)
#   5. CONSTRUCTOR — Python/Go/JS values, hashability, merges
#   6. REPRESENTER/DUMPER — irreversible presentation loss
#
# PARSERS UNDER TEST (answer for EACH unless a question says otherwise):
#   P11   = PyYAML yaml.safe_load          (YAML 1.1 resolver; last-key-wins)
#   P11C  = PyYAML CSafeLoader             (libyaml C; same schema, syntax edges)
#   P11F  = PyYAML FullLoader              (still no arbitrary Python objects;
#                                           more YAML-types-repo tags)
#   P11U  = PyYAML unsafe Loader/load()    (the RCE museum — sandbox only)
#   R12   = ruamel.yaml typ='safe' ~1.2    (core-ish; often re-adds merge keys)
#   R12D  = ruamel + DuplicateKeyError
#   R12RT = ruamel typ='rt'                (round-trip; preserves some presentation)
#   G12   = Go gopkg.in/yaml.v3            (1.2 core; key uniqueness often strict)
#   G12u  = Go yaml.v2                     (1.1-ish legacy — still in the wild)
#   JS    = YAML 1.2 JSON Schema mode
#   FS    = YAML 1.2 Failsafe              (ONLY map / seq / str)
#   CORE  = YAML 1.2 Core Schema           (spec-ideal; not any one library)
#   SPEC11= YAML 1.1 specification ideal   (not PyYAML — grade the DELTA)
#
# HOST LANGUAGE TRAPS (PhD required — "YAML did it" is insufficient):
#   PY-EQ  Python dict key equality: True == 1 == 1.0, False == 0,
#          and float('nan') != float('nan'). These are NOT YAML collisions;
#          they are host-runtime collisions AFTER successful construction.
#   JS-NUM IEEE-754 Number (no ints); keys always strings in JSON.stringify.
#   GO-MAP typed maps reject heterogeneous keys; interface{} maps are slippery.
#
# SCORING
#   +1   correct type+value for one parser
#   +3   correctly name the pipeline STAGE that diverges
#   +5   correct key-count including HOST-language collisions (mark PY-EQ)
#   +8   identity graph: which aliases share id() / pointer equality
#   +13  formal differential table (FS / JS / CORE / SPEC11 / P11) with zero misses
#   +21  construct a document meeting a multi-parser cardinality constraint
#   -5   missed silent key collision
#   -10  claimed two parsers "basically the same"
#   -15  cited v3 grading folklore without re-verification
#   -25  treated SPEC11 and P11 as identical for bools OR floats
#   INSTANT FAIL: "YAML is simple" / "just use JSON" without tears
#   INSTANT FAIL: "1e3 is obviously a float" without running a REPL
#
# METHOD REQUIREMENT
#   Every non-trivial claim needs: (parser, version, loader class, result, stage).
#   "I think" is not a citation. Paste REPL output or fail the oral.
#
# Time limit: one production outage, one dissertation chapter, or until tenure.
# =============================================================================


# %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
# CHAPTER 0 — DIRECTIVES, TAG HANDLES, AND THE LIE OF VERSION NEGOTIATION
# %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
#
# Real %YAML / %TAG lines are commented so this file still loads as one
# primary document (plus intentional multi-docs later). Mentally uncomment.
#
# Q0.1  File begins with `%YAML 1.2` but you call P11 safe_load.
#       (a) Which schema actually runs?
#       (b) At which pipeline STAGE is the directive consumed or ignored?
#       (c) Does P11C differ from pure P11 here?
#
# Q0.2  `%TAG !e! tag:example.com,2026:` then `foo: !e!widget bar`
#       Resolved tag URI on foo's value? Primary vs secondary tag handle rules?
#
# Q0.3  `%TAG !! tag:example.com,2026:`  (rebind the PRIMARY handle !!)
#       Then `x: !!str yes`. Is the value a string, or tag:example.com,2026:str?
#       Does safe_load allow rebinding !! ?
#
# Q0.4  Second document starts with `%YAML 1.1` after a first document said 1.2.
#       Spec: are schemas allowed to change mid-stream?
#       P11: does anything actually change?
#
# Q0.5  Place `%YAML 1.2` AFTER `---` (illegal).
#       P11 / G12 / R12: error, warn, or plain scalar beginning with %?
#       Name the stage.
#
# Q0.6  Two TAG directives define the same handle. Spec violation —
#       ComposerError? ParserError? Last-wins? Silent?
#
# Q0.7  PROVE or REFUTE: "If every implementation honored %YAML, Norway
#       would be fixed by writing %YAML 1.2 at the top of Helm charts."
#       Your answer must address (i) directive support, (ii) merge keys,
#       (iii) ecosystem inertia.
#
# %YAML 1.2
# %TAG !e! tag:example.com,2026:
# ---


# %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
# CHAPTER 1 — NORWAY INDUSTRIAL COMPLEX (WITH HOST-LANGUAGE FALLOUT)
# %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
#
# Q1.1  Type+value of EVERY sequence element under P11, R12/CORE, FS, SPEC11.
#       Four columns. SPEC11 ≠ P11 for y/n — if you merge those columns, -25.
#
# Q1.2  After P11 load, how many DISTINCT bool True values (value equality)?
# Q1.3  Is element index 0 (NO) the same Python type as index 6 ("NO")?
# Q1.4  Map length under: P11 | SPEC11 | R12/CORE | R12D | FS | G12.
#       List the actual P11 keys before peeking at any key at the bottom.
# Q1.5  P11: country_lookup[False]? [True]? ['y']? ['n']? ['oN']?
# Q1.6  R12: country_lookup["NO"] — quoted or unquoted winner? (last-wins)
# Q1.7  FS: are true / True / YES all plain strings AND all distinct keys?
# Q1.8  PY-EQ BOMB: after P11 load, evaluate:
#         country_lookup[1]     # you never wrote the key 1
#         country_lookup[1.0]
#         country_lookup[0]
#         country_lookup[False]
#       Explain using Python's bool-as-int subclass relationship.
#       At which stage does this collision appear? (Hint: not the resolver.)
# Q1.9  G12: unmarshal into map[string]interface{} vs map[interface{}]interface{}.
#       Which bool-key forms survive? Which become string keys?
# Q1.10 Oral: why did PyYAML DROP y|Y|n|N from the 1.1 bool regex while
#       keeping yes/no/on/off? Cite the operational failure mode (CI, locale,
#       single-letter country codes are NOT the full story — dig).

countries_i_have_visited:
  - NO
  - YE
  - on
  - off
  - n
  - y
  - "NO"
  - NO
  - true
  - True
  - TRUE
  - yes
  - Yes
  - YES
  - On
  - oN              # mixed case — in P11 regex? in SPEC11?
  - nO
  - YeS
  - OFF
  - Off
  - oFf
  - Y               # SPEC11 bool; P11?
  - N
  - ON
  - FALSE

country_lookup:
  NO: unquoted-NO
  YE: Yemen
  on: Ontario
  off: feature-off
  n: letter-n
  y: letter-y
  "NO": quoted-NO
  true: lower-true
  True: title-true
  TRUE: upper-true
  yes: lower-yes
  Yes: title-yes
  YES: upper-yes
  On: title-on
  oN: mixed-on
  OFF: upper-off
  false: lower-false
  False: title-false
  FALSE: upper-false
  # Q1.11  Mentally insert these two lines and recompute P11 key count:
  # 1: integer-one
